### Circuit Breaker with fallback implementation and metrics using a separate thread
In this design, we will implement a circuit breaker pattern that can be used to protect against failure of an external service. The circuit breaker will maintain a count of failures and when the threshold is reached, it will stop allowing commands to execute until the timeout has expired. When a command executes successfully, the circuit breaker will reset its state. Additionally, we will provide a fallback implementation that can be used in case the external service is not available. We will also use a separate thread to monitor the circuit breaker and trigger the fallback implementation when necessary.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.Callable;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class CircuitBreaker {
    private final Callable<String> callable;
    private int failures = 0;
    private final int threshold;
    private final long timeout;
    private final Thread monitorThread;

    public CircuitBreaker(Callable<String> callable, int threshold, long timeout) {
        this.callable = callable;
        this.threshold = threshold;
        this.timeout = timeout;
        monitorThread = new MonitorThread();
    }

    public String execute() throws Exception {
        if (failures >= threshold) {
            System.out.println("Circuit breaker triggered");
            try {
                Thread.sleep(timeout);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Circuit breaker interrupted", e);
            }
        }
        try {
            String response = callable.call();
            failures = 0;
            return response;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            failures++;
            System.out.println("Command failed");
            return "fallback";
        }
    }

    private class MonitorThread extends Thread {
        @Override
        public void run() {
            while (true) {
                if (failures >= threshold) {
                    // Fall back to the fallback implementation
                    callable.call();
                    failures = 0;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
