['Here is an example implementation of a modular Rust function named `distributed_data_aggregator`:
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::Arc;
use async_std::task;
use futures::stream::{StreamExt, BoxStream};
use anyhow::Result;

struct DataAggregator {
    data_sources: HashMap<String, BoxStream>,
}

impl DataAggregator {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            data_sources: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    pub async fn add_data_source(&mut self, name: &str, source: BoxStream) {
        self.data_sources.insert(name.to_string(), source);
    }

    pub async fn remove_data_source(&mut self, name: &str) -> Result<()> {
        if !self.data_sources.contains_key(name) {
            return Err(anyhow!("Data source not found"));
        }

        self.data_sources.remove(name);
        Ok(())
    }

    pub async fn process_data(&mut self, data: Vec<u8>) -> Result<()> {
        for (_, source) in &self.data_sources {
            let data = source.take();
            task::spawn(async move {
                match data {
                    Ok(Some(data)) => {
                        // process data here
                    }
                    Ok(None) => {
                        // no more data from this source, remove it
                        self.remove_data_source(name).await?;
                    }
                    Err(e) => {
                        // handle error here
                    }
                }
            });
        }

        Ok(())
    }
}
```
This implementation uses a `HashMap` to store the data sources, with each entry containing a name and a stream of data. It also provides methods for adding and removing data sources dynamically.

The `process_data` method takes a vector of bytes as input, which is processed by each data source in parallel using a separate task. The tasks are created using the `task::spawn` function from the `async-std` crate, which allows us to run them concurrently without blocking the main thread.

The code also includes error handling using the `anyhow` library, which provides a flexible and extensible way of handling errors in asynchronous Rust code.

It's worth noting that this is just an example implementation, and the actual implementation will depend on the specific requirements of the system being built.']

