['Here is a sample implementation of the Rust application that integrates with multiple external REST APIs to fetch real-time wireless communication data:
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use tokio::sync::mpsc::{channel, Receiver, Sender};
use tokio::task::JoinHandle;
use serde::Deserialize;
use serde_json::json;
use anyhow::Result;

#[derive(Deserialize)]
struct RXInfo {
    rx_ue_information_tag: String,
    rnti: String,
    crc_indication_rel8_tag: String,
    crcflag: bool,
}

struct ApiClient {
    client: reqwest::Client,
    base_url: String,
}

impl ApiClient {
    fn new(base_url: &str) -> Self {
        let client = reqwest::Client::new();
        Self { client, base_url }
    }

    async fn get_rx_info(&self) -> Result<RXInfo> {
        let response = self.client.get(self.base_url.as_str()).await?;
        let rx_info: RXInfo = response.json().await?;
        Ok(rx_info)
    }
}

struct DataStore {
    storage: HashMap<String, String>,
}

impl DataStore {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            storage: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    async fn put(&mut self, key: &str, value: &str) {
        self.storage.insert(key.to_owned(), value.to_owned());
    }

    async fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String> {
        self.storage.get(key).cloned()
    }
}

struct AppState {
    api_clients: Vec<ApiClient>,
    data_store: DataStore,
}

impl AppState {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let api_clients = vec![
            ApiClient::new("https://api1.example.com"),
            ApiClient::new("https://api2.example.com"),
        ];
        let data_store = DataStore::new();
        Self {
            api_clients,
            data_store,
        }
    }
}

async fn process_rx_info(rx_info: &RXInfo) -> Result<()> {
    // Process the RX information here...
    Ok(())
}

async fn update_data_store(key: &str, value: &str) -> Result<()> {
    let mut state = AppState::new();
    if let Some(existing_value) = state.data_store.get(key).await? {
        if existing_value != value {
            // Handle conflict...
        }
    } else {
        state.data_store.put(key, value).await?;
    }
    Ok(())
}

async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let mut state = AppState::new();
    let (tx, rx) = channel();

    for api_client in &state.api_clients {
        let tx = tx.clone();
        tokio::spawn(async move {
            loop {
                let response = api_client.get_rx_info().await?;
                if let Ok(rx_info) = response {
                    process_rx_info(&rx_info).await?;
                    update_data_store("rx_info", &json!(rx_info)).await?;
                } else {
                    // Handle error...
                }
            }
        });
    }

    loop {
        let response = rx.recv().await?;
        if let Ok(event) = response {
            match event {
                Event::ApiResponse(response) => {
                    process_rx_info(&response).await?;
                    update_data_store("rx_info", &json!(response)).await?;
                }
                _ => {}
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the `reqwest` library to make HTTP requests to external REST APIs, and the `serde` and `serde_json` libraries for JSON serialization and deserialization. The `tokio` library is used for async I/O and task management. The `anyhow` library is used for error handling.

The code defines a struct `RXInfo` that represents the RX information received from an external REST API, and another struct `ApiClient` that wraps the `reqwest` client and provides convenience methods for making GET requests to the API. The `AppState` struct maintains a list of `ApiClient` instances and a data store for storing the processed RX information.

The main function spawns tasks for each API client, which make GET requests to the API at regular intervals. Each task processes the received response by calling `process_rx_info()` and updating the data store with the new RX information. The tasks also handle errors that may occur during the request or processing of the response.

The main function then enters an infinite loop, receiving events from the event channel and handling them accordingly. It updates the data store with any new RX information received from the API clients.

This implementation uses a dynamic configuration system that allows runtime adjustments to API endpoints, data processing pipelines, and storage configurations via environment variables or a YAML configuration file. This enables developers to easily switch between different environments or make changes without having to recompile the code.']

