Here is a possible implementation of the rate-limiting and request queuing system using Node.js:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const limiter = require('rate-limiter')(app);

// Set up rate limiting for each client IP address
limiter.setMaxRequestsPerMinute(500, 'ip');
limiter.setTolerance(20); // tolerate up to 20% variance in traffic distribution among services

// Set up request queuing for each service
const queue = new Queue();

// Handle incoming requests and enqueue them for processing
app.post('/request', async (req, res) => {
  const requestId = await queue.enqueue(req);
  res.send(`Request ${requestId} queued`);
});

// Process requests in the queue asynchronously
setInterval(() => {
  if (queue.length > 0) {
    const req = queue.dequeue();
    // Handle request here
    console.log(`Processing request ${req.id}`);
    // Respond to client with success status code
    res.sendStatus(200);
  }
}, 1000); // process requests every second

// Back-pressure mechanism to prevent overwhelming downstream services
const backPressure = new BackPressure();
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  if (backPressure.isOverloaded()) {
    res.sendStatus(503); // service unavailable
  } else {
    next();
  }
});

// Monitoring and logging capabilities
const logger = require('morgan');
app.use(logger('dev'));

// Start the server
const port = 80;
app.listen(port, () => console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`));
```
This implementation uses the `express` framework for building the web server and the `rate-limiter` module for rate limiting incoming requests based on client IP address. The `queue` object is used to enqueue incoming requests for processing, and a back-pressure mechanism is implemented using the `BackPressure` class to prevent overwhelming downstream services. The server also uses the `morgan` logger module to provide detailed logging of incoming requests and their responses.

