Here is an example implementation of the `addSiteToDirectory` function:
```csharp
public void AddSiteToDirectory(string directoryPath, string siteName) {
  // Check if web.config file exists in the directory
  if (!File.Exists($"{directoryPath}\\web.config")) {
    return null;
  }

  // Create a new website in the directory
  var website = new Website();
  website.Name = siteName;
  website.PhysicalPath = $"{directoryPath}\\{siteName}";

  // Add the website to the IIS application pool
  var appPool = new ApplicationPool();
  appPool.Name = "DefaultAppPool";
  appPool.Recycling.Disabled = true;
  appPool.Add(website);

  return website;
}
```
This function takes two arguments: `directoryPath` and `siteName`. It checks if a `web.config` file exists in the directory using the `File.Exists` method, and returns `null` if it does not exist. If it does exist, the function creates a new website object with the given site name and physical path, and adds it to the IIS application pool using the `ApplicationPool` class. The function also disables recycling for the default application pool using the `Recycling` property of the `ApplicationPool` object.']

