Here is a possible implementation of the `validateOAuthToken` function in C++:
```
#include <json/json.h>

bool validateOAuthToken(const std::string& token) {
    Json::Value root;
    Json::Reader reader;
    if (!reader.parse(token, root)) {
        return false;
    }

    // Verify mandatory fields are present
    if (!root["access_token"].isString() || !root["expires_in"].isNumber() || !root["token_type"].isString()) {
        return false;
    }

    // Validate token type is 'Bearer'
    if (root["token_type"].asString() != "Bearer") {
        return false;
    }

    // Verify expires in is a positive integer
    int expiresIn = root["expires_in"].asInt();
    if (expiresIn <= 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // Verify refresh token is present if specified
    if (root.isMember("refresh_token") && !root["refresh_token"].isString()) {
        return false;
    }

    return true;
}
```
This function takes a JSON string representing an OAuth2 access token response and verifies its validity according to the specified criteria. It first parses the JSON string using the `Json::Reader` class, which is part of the `jsoncpp` library. If the parse fails, it returns `false`.

Then, it checks that all mandatory fields are present in the JSON object: `access_token`, `expires_in`, and `token_type`. If any of these fields are missing or have a wrong type, the function returns `false`.

Next, it verifies that the `token_type` is set to `'Bearer'`. If it is not, the function returns `false`.

Finally, it checks that the `expires_in` field is a positive integer. If it is not, or if it has a wrong type, the function returns `false`.

If all of these verifications are successful, the function returns `true`.

This implementation uses the `Json::Value` class from the `jsoncpp` library to represent JSON values, and the `isMember` method to check if a field is present in the JSON object. It also uses the `asString` and `asInt` methods to get the value of a field as a string or an integer, respectively.

Note that this implementation does not handle refresh tokens, but it can be easily modified to do so by adding additional checks for the presence and type of the `refresh_token` field.']

