#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <atomic>

class EventProcessor {
public:
    // Constructor
    EventProcessor(int numThreads) : numThreads_(numThreads), stop_(false) {}

    // Destructor
    ~EventProcessor() { stop(); }

    // Start processing events
    void start() {
        for (int i = 0; i < numThreads_; ++i) {
            threads_.emplace_back([this]() { processEvents(); });
        }
    }

    // Stop processing events
    void stop() {
        if (!stop_) {
            stop_ = true;
            cv_.notify_all();
            for (auto& t : threads_) {
                t.join();
            }
            threads_.clear();
        }
    }

    // Add an event to the processing queue
    void addEvent(const std::string& event) {
        events_.push_back(event);
        cv_.notify_one();
    }

private:
    // Process events in a separate thread
    void processEvents() {
        while (!stop_) {
            // Wait for new events to be added to the queue
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mux_);
            cv_.wait(lock, [this]() { return !events_.empty() || stop_; });

            if (stop_) {
                break;
            }

            // Process events in batches of 10
            std::vector<std::string> events;
            for (int i = 0; i < 10 && !events_.empty(); ++i) {
                events.push_back(events_.front());
                events_.pop_front();
            }

            // Do something with the events
            // ...
        }
    }

    std::mutex mux_;
    std::condition_variable cv_;
    std::vector<std::thread> threads_;
    std::deque<std::string> events_;
    bool stop_;
    int numThreads_;
};
```
This is a simple implementation of an event processor that uses multiple threads to process events in parallel. The `EventProcessor` class maintains a queue of events, and each thread pulls events from the queue and processes them in batches of 10. The `start()` method starts the processing threads, while the `stop()` method stops them. The `addEvent()` method adds an event to the processing queue, and the `processEvents()` method waits for new events to be added to the queue before processing them.

The implementation also includes a mutex and condition variable to ensure that only one thread can access the queue at a time, and a flag to signal when to stop processing events. The number of threads used by the processor is specified in the constructor.

This is just a basic example of how to implement an event processor, and there are many ways to optimize and improve it depending on your specific use case. For example, you could use a thread pool instead of creating new threads each time an event is added, or use a more efficient data structure for the event queue (such as a circular buffer). Additionally, you would need to add error handling and recovery mechanisms to handle failures and ensure that the system remains operational.']

