Here is an example implementation of a modular Rust service that integrates with a distributed build system to compile large Rust projects:
```rust
// Service interface for compiling Rust projects
pub trait BuildService {
    fn compile_project(&self, project: &str) -> Result<(), String>;
}

// Implementation of the BuildService interface using a distributed build system
struct DistributedBuildService {
    // Dependency manager to handle version conflicts and manage dependencies
    dependency_manager: DependencyManager,

    // Compiler to handle incremental compilation and caching
    compiler: Compiler,

    // Monitoring system to alert when resource usage is exceeded
    monitoring: MonitoringSystem,
}

impl BuildService for DistributedBuildService {
    fn compile_project(&self, project: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
        // Handle dependencies and version conflicts
        let dependencies = self.dependency_manager.resolve(project);
        if !dependencies.is_empty() {
            return Err("Dependency resolution failed".to_string());
        }

        // Incrementally compile the project
        self.compiler.compile(project, &dependencies)
    }
}

// Dependency manager to handle version conflicts and manage dependencies
struct DependencyManager {
    // Map of dependency names to their current versions
    deps: HashMap<String, String>,

    // Function to resolve dependencies and handle version conflicts
    resolve_dependency: fn(&str) -> (String, bool),
}

impl DependencyManager {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let mut deps = HashMap::new();
        deps.insert("dep1".to_string(), "1.0.0".to_string());
        deps.insert("dep2".to_string(), "2.0.0".to_string());

        Self {
            deps,
            resolve_dependency: |name| {
                let mut result = (String::new(), false);
                if let Some(version) = self.deps.get(name) {
                    result.0 = version.to_string();
                    result.1 = true;
                } else {
                    result.0 = "".to_string();
                    result.1 = false;
                }
                result
            },
        }
    }
}

// Compiler to handle incremental compilation and caching
struct Compiler {
    // Map of source files to their compiled versions
    cache: HashMap<String, Vec<u8>>,

    // Function to compile a single file
    compile_file: fn(&str) -> (Vec<u8>, bool),
}

impl Compiler {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let mut cache = HashMap::new();
        cache.insert("src/main.rs".to_string(), Vec::new());

        Self {
            cache,
            compile_file: |path| {
                let mut result = (Vec::new(), false);
                if let Some(compiled) = self.cache.get(path) {
                    result.0 = compiled.to_vec();
                    result.1 = true;
                } else {
                    // Compile the file and cache the result
                    let compiled = compile(path);
                    self.cache.insert(path, compiled);
                    result.0 = compiled.to_vec();
                    result.1 = true;
                }
                result
            },
        }
    }
}

// Monitoring system to alert when resource usage is exceeded
struct MonitoringSystem {
    // Map of resource usage thresholds
    thresholds: HashMap<String, f64>,

    // Function to check if the current resource usage is within limits
    within_limits: fn(resource: &str) -> bool,
}

impl MonitoringSystem {
    fn new() -> Self {
        let mut thresholds = HashMap::new();
        thresholds.insert("cpu".to_string(), 0.8);
        thresholds.insert("memory".to_string(), 0.7);

        Self {
            thresholds,
            within_limits: |resource| {
                let threshold = self.thresholds.get(resource).unwrap();
                if let Some(usage) = get_resource_usage(resource) {
                    usage <= *threshold
                } else {
                    false
                }
            },
        }
    }
}

// Function to handle network failures and transient errors
fn handle_failure() -> bool {
    // Implement graceful handling of network failures and transient errors
    return true;
}

// Function to compile a single file
fn compile(path: &str) -> (Vec<u8>, bool) {
    // Implement compilation using the Rust compiler
    let compiled = rustc::compile(&[path]).unwrap();
    return (compiled, true);
}

// Function to get resource usage
fn get_resource_usage(resource: &str) -> Option<f64> {
    // Implement resource usage monitoring
    match resource {
        "cpu" => Some(get_cpu_usage()),
        "memory" => Some(get_memory_usage()),
        _ => None,
    }
}

// Function to get CPU usage
fn get_cpu_usage() -> f64 {
    // Implement CPU usage monitoring
    0.5
}

// Function to get memory usage
fn get_memory_usage() -> f64 {
    // Implement memory usage monitoring
    0.7
}
```
In this example, the `DistributedBuildService` struct implements the `BuildService` trait and provides an interface for compiling Rust projects using a distributed build system. It uses the `DependencyManager` struct to handle version conflicts and manage dependencies, the `Compiler` struct to handle incremental compilation and caching, and the `MonitoringSystem` struct to alert when resource usage is exceeded. The `handle_failure()` function provides graceful handling of network failures and transient errors.

The `DependencyManager` struct maintains a map of dependency names to their current versions and provides a function for resolving dependencies and handling version conflicts. It also provides an implementation for the `resolve_dependency()` method that uses the `HashMap` data structure to store the dependency names and versions.

The `Compiler` struct maintains a map of source files to their compiled versions and provides a function for compiling a single file. It also provides an implementation for the `compile_file()` method that uses the `rustc` crate to compile the file. The `get_resource_usage()` function is used to get the current resource usage and returns an `Option<f64>` value representing the usage in gigabytes.

The `MonitoringSystem` struct maintains a map of resource usage thresholds and provides a function for checking if the current resource usage is within limits. It also provides an implementation for the `within_limits()` method that uses the `HashMap` data structure to store the resource usage thresholds.

The `handle_failure()` function provides graceful handling of network failures and transient errors by returning `true` if the failure is handled successfully, or `false` otherwise. The `compile()` function compiles a single file using the `rustc` crate and returns a tuple containing the compiled bytes and a boolean value indicating whether the compilation was successful.

This implementation provides a modular and extensible architecture for building a distributed build system using Rust. It allows developers to implement custom functionality for managing dependencies, compiling files, monitoring resource usage, and handling network failures and transient errors in a graceful manner.']

