The `TextProcessor` class should be implemented using a chain of responsibility pattern. Each handler in the chain will be responsible for processing a specific type of text, such as detecting canonical references or validating external API responses. The handlers can be added to the chain dynamically, allowing new types of text processing to be added without modifying the existing codebase.

Here is an example implementation of the `TextProcessor` class using a chain of responsibility pattern:
```typescript
import { TextProcessor } from './text-processor';
import { Handler } from './handler';

// Define a list of pre-defined handlers for processing text
const handlers = [
  new CanonicalReferenceHandler(),
  new ExternalAPIHandler()
];

export class TextProcessor implements Handler {
  // Implement the handle method to process text
  public async handle(text: string): Promise<string> {
    return await this.processText(text);
  }

  // Define a private method for processing text using handlers
  private async processText(text: string): Promise<string> {
    // Create an instance of the chain of responsibility pattern
    const chain = new HandlerChain();

    // Add the pre-defined handlers to the chain
    handlers.forEach(handler => {
      chain.addHandler(handler);
    });

    // Process the text using the chain of responsibility pattern
    return await chain.handle(text);
  }
}
