['Here is an example implementation of a C++ function that modifies a given `std::forward_list<std::string>` in place:
```c
void insertAfter(const std::string& key, const std::string& value, std::forward_list<std::string>& list) {
    auto iter = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), key);
    if (iter != list.end()) {
        // Insert the new value after the first occurrence of the key
        iter = list.insert_after(iter, value);
    } else {
        // Insert the new value at the end of the list
        list.push_back(value);
    }
}
```
This function takes three arguments: `key`, which is the string to search for in the list; `value`, which is the string to insert after `key` or at the end of the list if it's not found; and `list`, which is the forward list to modify. It uses the `std::find()` algorithm to find the first occurrence of `key` in the list, and then inserts `value` after that element using the `std::forward_list<std::string>::insert_after()` method. If `key` is not found, it inserts `value` at the end of the list using the `std::forward_list<std::string>::push_back()` method.

Here's an example usage of this function:
```c
int main() {
    std::forward_list<std::string> list = {"apple", "banana", "cherry"};
    insertAfter("apple", "orange", list);
    for (auto& s : list) {
        std::cout << s << ' ';
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This will output `apple orange banana cherry`. If the function is called with `insertAfter("banana", "pear", list)`, it will insert `"pear"` after the first occurrence of `"banana"` in the list, and the output will be `apple orange pear cherry`.']

