### Implementation details

The `FileUploaderClient` library is designed to be highly modular and flexible, allowing users to easily integrate with different cloud storage providers without requiring code changes. The library uses the `CloudProvider` interface as a base class for all supported cloud storage providers, which allows for interoperability between different cloud providers.

The library also includes a `RetryPolicy` class that handles retries based on error type and prioritizes retries for transient errors. The retry policy can be configured with different parameters such as the maximum number of retries, the delay between retries, and an array of error codes to retry.

The library also includes a `UploadProgressTracker` class that allows users to track the progress of their uploads in real-time. The tracker uses the `totalSize` parameter to calculate the percentage of the file that has been uploaded so far, and provides an array of `currentBytes` values for each individual chunk of the file that has been uploaded.

Finally, the library includes a `MessageQueue` class that allows users to handle bursty workloads gracefully by processing messages asynchronously. The queue uses a FIFO (First-In-First-Out) strategy to ensure that messages are processed in the order they were added, and provides an API for adding new messages, processing existing messages, and removing processed messages.

### Performance optimization

To optimize performance, the library uses async/await syntax throughout, allowing for more efficient handling of asynchronous operations such as file uploads. The library also includes a `Promise` wrapper around each cloud provider's API, which allows for more efficient handling of concurrent requests and retries.

The library also includes a `MessageQueue` class that handles messages asynchronously, allowing for more efficient handling of bursty workloads. The queue uses a FIFO (First-In-First-Out) strategy to ensure that messages are processed in the order they were added, and provides an API for adding new messages, processing existing messages, and removing processed messages.

### Security considerations

The `FileUploaderClient` library takes security seriously, and includes a number of measures to prevent unauthorized access to your files. The library uses SSL/TLS encryption for all HTTPS requests, and provides an API for generating signed URLs that allow users to securely upload files directly to their own cloud storage providers without requiring additional authentication steps.

The library also includes a number of error handling measures to prevent unauthorized access to your cloud storage accounts. For example, the library will automatically retry failed requests a certain number of times before reporting an error, and provides an API for specifying custom error handlers that can be used to handle errors in a more controlled manner.

### Testing

The `FileUploaderClient` library includes a comprehensive test suite that covers all major use cases, including successful uploads, failed uploads, and edge cases such as invalid file types or malformed URLs. The tests are designed to be flexible and can be easily extended or modified to meet the specific needs of your project.

### Deployment

