    // Method for updating the configuration file
    void updateConfigFile();

    // Method for checking the health of a specific resource
    void checkResourceHealth(const ResourceDescriptor& resource);

    // Method for handling errors during asynchronous checks
    void handleAsyncCheckError(const std::string& errorMessage);
};

class ResourceDescriptor {
public:
    // Constructor
    ResourceDescriptor(const std::string& connectionDetails, const std::string& authenticationMethod, const std::string& performanceThreshold);

    // Connection details
    const std::string& getConnectionDetails() const;

    // Authentication method
    const std::string& getAuthenticationMethod() const;

    // Performance threshold
    const std::string& getPerformanceThreshold() const;
};

class NotificationManager {
public:
    // Constructor
    NotificationManager();

    // Destructor
    ~NotificationManager();

    // Method for sending notifications to stakeholders
    void sendNotifications(const std::string& notificationType, const std::vector<std::string>& resourceNames);
};
```

This is a basic outline of the `NetworkHealthChecker` class and its dependencies. The `NotificationManager` class is used to manage notifications sent to stakeholders when an issue with a monitored resource is detected. The `ResourceDescriptor` class provides information about each resource, including connection details, authentication method, and performance threshold. The `NetworkHealthChecker` class takes in a configuration file path upon construction and can dynamically update the configuration using the `updateConfigFile()` method.

The `checkResourceHealth()` method checks the health of a specific resource by making an asynchronous HTTP request to the resource's endpoint and handling any errors that may occur during the check. The method also maintains a map of resource descriptors for easy access to information about each resource.

The `start()` method starts the health checker by creating a thread pool for the asynchronous checks and initializing the resource descriptors map with the configuration file data. The `stop()` method stops the health checker by joining all threads in the thread pool and clearing any remaining resources in the map.

The `handleAsyncCheckError()` method is used to handle errors that may occur during asynchronous checks, such as API rate limiting or authentication failures. This method can be customized to handle different error scenarios based on the needs of the project.

This implementation provides a modular and scalable architecture for monitoring the health and performance of diverse network resources. It also includes sophisticated error handling logic to manage varying network conditions and maintain overall service availability metrics. Additionally, it addresses potential scalability challenges by designing the checker to operate effectively across distributed environments with minimal network latency and data transmission overhead."]

