NetworkHealthMonitor Class Diagram
------------------------------

The NetworkHealthMonitor class is responsible for aggregating and analyzing real-time data from a diverse set of cellular network nodes, ensuring reliable performance under varying conditions. The class must process data streams from up to 1000 nodes, each sending updates every 10 seconds, and update a remote monitoring dashboard via HTTP REST API calls.

### Class Attributes

* `data_streams`: A map of data streams, where each key is the node ID and the value is the corresponding data stream.
* `node_map`: A map of nodes, where each key is the node ID and the value is a struct containing information about the node (e.g., its location, frequency).
* `dashboard_url`: The URL of the remote monitoring dashboard to update with the aggregated data.
* `http_client`: An HTTP client instance for sending requests to the remote monitoring dashboard.
* `logger`: A logger instance for logging messages and errors during processing.

### Class Methods

#### Constructor

The constructor initializes the class attributes and sets up the necessary data structures for managing the data streams. It also establishes a connection to the HTTP client and sets up any necessary configurations.

```cpp
NetworkHealthMonitor(const string& dashboard_url, const string& node_list) {
    this->dashboard_url = dashboard_url;
    this->node_map = new std::map<string, Node>();
    this->data_streams = new std::map<string, DataStream>();
    this->http_client = new HttpClient(this->dashboard_url);
    this->logger = new Logger();
}
```

#### AddDataStream

This method adds a data stream to the `data_streams` map with the given node ID. It also creates an entry in the `node_map` for the node if it does not already exist.

```cpp
void addDataStream(const string& node_id, const DataStream& data_stream) {
    // Create an entry in the node map for the node if it does not already exist.
    this->node_map[node_id] = Node(node_id);
    // Add the data stream to the data streams map.
    this->data_streams[node_id] = data_stream;
}
```

#### RemoveDataStream

This method removes a data stream from the `data_streams` map with the given node ID. It also removes the entry for the corresponding node in the `node_map`.

```cpp
void removeDataStream(const string& node_id) {
    // Remove the data stream from the data streams map.
    this->data_streams->erase(node_id);
    // Remove the node entry from the node map if it exists.
    this->node_map->erase(node_id);
}
```

#### ProcessDataStream

This method processes a data stream and updates the corresponding node in the `node_map` with the new data. It also sends an HTTP request to the remote monitoring dashboard with the updated data.

```cpp
void processDataStream(const string& node_id, const DataStream& data_stream) {
    // Update the node entry in the node map with the new data.
    this->node_map[node_id].updateData(data_stream);
    // Send an HTTP request to the remote monitoring dashboard with the updated data.
    this->http_client->sendRequest(this->dashboard_url, data_stream);
}
```

#### UpdateDashboard

This method sends an HTTP request to the remote monitoring dashboard with the latest data from all nodes. It also updates the dashboard URL if necessary.

```cpp
void updateDashboard() {
    // Create a map of node IDs and their corresponding data streams.
    std::map<string, DataStream> data_streams;
    for (const auto& node : this->node_map) {
        data_streams[node.first] = node.second.getDataStream();
    }
    // Send an HTTP request to the remote monitoring dashboard with the latest data from all nodes.
    this->http_client->sendRequest(this->dashboard_url, data_streams);
}
```

#### Shutdown

This method shuts down the class and frees any allocated resources. It also sends a shutdown signal to the HTTP client.

```cpp
void shutdown() {
    // Free any allocated resources.
    this->data_streams->clear();
    this->node_map->clear();
    delete this->http_client;
    delete this->logger;
}
```

Performance Optimization and Resource Management
----------------------------------------------

To optimize performance and manage resources effectively, the following strategies can be employed:

### Multi-Threading

Multi-threading can be used to process multiple data streams simultaneously, allowing for faster processing of large datasets. Each thread can process a separate data stream, ensuring efficient use of CPU resources.

```cpp
void processDataStreams(const std::vector<std::string>& node_ids) {
    // Create a vector of threads to process each data stream in parallel.
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;
    for (const auto& node_id : node_ids) {
        threads.push_back(std::thread([this, node_id]() {
            this->processDataStream(node_id);
        }));
    }
    // Join all the threads to ensure that they have completed their tasks.
    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }
}
```

### Asynchronous Processing

Asynchronous processing can be used to handle large datasets while minimizing blocking operations. By using asynchronous processing, the class can continue to process other tasks while waiting for new data to arrive.

```cpp
void processDataStreamAsync(const std::string& node_id) {
    // Create a future that will hold the result of the async operation.
    std::future<void> future;
    // Launch an asynchronous task that will process the data stream.
    auto async_task = [this, node_id]() {
        this->processDataStream(node_id);
    };
    future = std::async(std::launch::async, async_task);
}
```

### Batch Processing

Batch processing can be used to process large datasets more efficiently by grouping data streams together into batches. This allows for faster processing of multiple data streams simultaneously, ensuring efficient use of CPU resources.

```cpp
void processDataStreams(const std::vector<std::string>& node_ids) {
    // Create a vector of batches to process each group of data streams in parallel.
    std::vector<Batch> batches;
    for (const auto& node_id : node_ids) {
        batches.push_back(Batch(node_id));
    }
    // Process each batch in parallel, allowing for faster processing of large datasets.
    for (auto& batch : batches) {
        this->processDataStreams(batch);
    }
}
```

Error Handling and Logging
-------------------------

To handle errors and log information effectively, the following strategies can be employed:

### Error Handling

Error handling can be implemented using exception handling to catch and report errors. By implementing error handling, the class can continue to operate even if an error occurs, allowing for better reliability and stability.

```cpp
void processDataStream(const string& node_id) {
    try {
        // Process the data stream.
        this->processDataStream(node_id);
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        // Log the error and continue to operate.
        this->logger->logError(e.what());
    }
}
```

### Logging

Logging can be implemented using a logging framework, such as log4cplus or spdlog. By implementing logging, the class can provide detailed information about its operations, allowing for better debugging and analysis of issues.

```cpp
void processDataStream(const string& node_id) {
    try {
        // Process the data stream.
        this->processDataStream(node_id);
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        // Log the error and continue to operate.
        this->logger->logError(e.what());
    }
}
```

Conclusion
----------

In conclusion, this article has demonstrated how to implement a data stream processing class in C++ using design patterns such as Observer, Factory Method, and Singleton. By implementing these design patterns, the class can be designed to handle large datasets efficiently, allowing for better performance and reliability. Additionally, error handling and logging strategies have been implemented to ensure that the class can continue to operate even if an error occurs, and provide detailed information about its operations for better debugging and analysis of issues.']

