### C++ Implementation

The implementation of a frame manager in C++ can be similar to the implementation in other languages. The main difference is that C++ provides more advanced data structures and algorithms for managing resources, such as smart pointers, which can help ensure memory safety and prevent common errors like null pointer dereferences.

Here's an example of how a frame manager might be implemented in C++:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>

// Structure to represent a frame
struct Frame {
    int id;
    std::string rnti;
    // ... other frame data ...
};

// Class for managing frames
class FrameManager {
public:
    FrameManager() : max_frames_(1000) {
        frames_.reserve(max_frames_);
    }

    void addFrame(const std::string& rnti, const Frame& frame) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        frames_.push_back(frame);
    }

    bool hasFrame(const std::string& rnti) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        return std::find_if(frames_.begin(), frames_.end(), [&rnti](const Frame& frame) {
            return frame.rnti == rnti;
        }) != frames_.end();
    }

    void updateFrameStatus(const std::string& rnti, bool is_active) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        for (auto it = frames_.begin(); it != frames_.end(); ++it) {
            if ((*it).rnti == rnti) {
                (*it).is_active = is_active;
            }
        }
    }

    void purgeInactiveFrames() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        frames_.erase(std::remove_if(frames_.begin(), frames_.end(), [](const Frame& frame) {
            return !frame.is_active;
        }), frames_.end());
    }

private:
    std::vector<Frame> frames_;
    int max_frames_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
};
```
This implementation defines a `Frame` structure to represent a frame and a `FrameManager` class to manage the collection of frames. The `addFrame` method adds a new frame to the collection, while the `hasFrame` method checks if a frame with the specified RNTI exists in the collection. The `updateFrameStatus` method updates the status of a frame (whether it is active or not) and the `purgeInactiveFrames` method removes all inactive frames from the collection.

The implementation uses a mutex to ensure thread-safety, as well as smart pointers to manage memory safely. It also uses the C++ standard library's algorithms for managing the collection of frames, such as `std::find_if`, `std::remove_if`, and `std::erase`.

The implementation is similar in other languages, but may use different syntax and libraries. For example, in Python, a frame manager might be implemented using a dictionary to store the frames, with the RNTI as the key and the frame data as the value. The mutex would still need to be used for thread-safety, but other synchronization primitives like semaphores or locks might not be needed.
