void insertAfterFirst(forward_list<string> &list, const string& target, const string& value) {
    // Find the first occurrence of the target string in the list
    auto it = find(list.begin(), list.end(), target);

    // If the target string is not found, insert the value at the end of the list
    if (it == list.end()) {
        list.push_back(value);
    } else {
        // Insert the value after the first occurrence of the target string
        list.insert_after(it, value);
    }
}
```
This modified version of the function accepts a forward list of strings as an argument and two string arguments `target` and `value`. The function finds the first occurrence of the `target` string in the list using the `find()` algorithm and inserts the `value` string after that occurrence. If the `target` string is not found, the `value` string is inserted at the end of the list.

The function modifies the original forward list by inserting or appending the `value` string as needed. The function does not return anything and instead modifies the original list in place.

This modified version of the function is useful when you need to insert a string into a forward list after the first occurrence of a specific string, while handling the case where the target string is not found. It is also memory-efficient as it only allocates the necessary memory for the new string and does not reallocate the entire list."]

