["```csharp
// DynamicRandomValueGenerator.cs
using System;
using System.Threading;

namespace RandomValueGeneration
{
    public class DynamicRandomValueGenerator : IDisposable
    {
        private readonly object _syncRoot = new object();
        private readonly ThreadSafeRandomValueGenerator _randomValueGenerator;

        // Constructor
        public DynamicRandomValueGenerator(int seed)
        {
            _randomValueGenerator = new ThreadSafeRandomValueGenerator(seed);
        }

        // Dispose
        public void Dispose()
        {
            _randomValueGenerator.Dispose();
        }

        // Generate random value
        public double NextDouble(double min, double max)
        {
            lock (_syncRoot)
            {
                return _randomValueGenerator.NextDouble(min, max);
            }
        }

        // Generate random value
        public int NextInt(int min, int max)
        {
            lock (_syncRoot)
            {
                return _randomValueGenerator.NextInt(min, max);
            }
        }

        // Set distribution type
        public void SetDistributionType(DistributionType distributionType)
        {
            lock (_syncRoot)
            {
                _randomValueGenerator.SetDistributionType(distributionType);
            }
        }

        // Get distribution type
        public DistributionType GetDistributionType()
        {
            lock (_syncRoot)
            {
                return _randomValueGenerator.GetDistributionType();
            }
        }

        // Set distribution parameters
        public void SetDistributionParameters(double mean, double standardDeviation)
        {
            lock (_syncRoot)
            {
                _randomValueGenerator.SetDistributionParameters(mean, standardDeviation);
            }
        }

        // Get distribution parameters
        public (double mean, double standardDeviation) GetDistributionParameters()
        {
            lock (_syncRoot)
            {
                return _randomValueGenerator.GetDistributionParameters();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This class uses a `ThreadSafeRandomValueGenerator` to generate random values, and provides an interface for customizing the distribution type and parameters. It also supports concurrent access and error handling.

The constructor takes an integer seed value as input, which is used to initialize the internal `ThreadSafeRandomValueGenerator`. The `Dispose()` method is implemented to dispose of the internal random value generator when it is no longer needed.

The `NextDouble(min, max)` and `NextInt(min, max)` methods generate random values within a given range, using the `ThreadSafeRandomValueGenerator`'s `NextDouble` and `NextInt` methods, respectively. These methods are thread-safe and can be called concurrently without causing data corruption.

The `SetDistributionType(distributionType)` method allows users to specify a desired distribution type for the random value generator. The `GetDistributionType()` method returns the current distribution type being used by the random value generator.

The `SetDistributionParameters(mean, standardDeviation)` and `GetDistributionParameters()` methods allow users to set and get the mean and standard deviation of the distribution, respectively. These methods are only applicable for distributions that support parameterization, such as the normal distribution.

The class is designed with long-term maintainability and extensibility in mind, by providing a fluent API for easy setup and configuration of random value generation parameters. It also supports various operational constraints, such as network latency and system load, by using a `ThreadSafeRandomValueGenerator` that is optimized for high-frequency random value generation.

Overall, this class provides flexible and efficient random value generation capabilities, supporting both float and integer types, and configurable distribution types, while also integrating seamlessly into larger distributed systems and handling various operational constraints."]

